# Findings in *history* that were reviewed and are not credentials.
#
# The Secrets workflow scans every commit reachable from the checked-out one,
# not just the current tree, so anything that was ever committed is scanned
# again on every run. These three are in commits that are already public and
# cannot be un-published; each is excused by its exact fingerprint
# (commit:file:rule:line), which matches that one hit and nothing else — not
# the file, not the rule, not the same string in a later commit.
#
# Adding a line here is the last step after a real leak, never the first:
# rotate the credential, then excuse the historical hit. A fingerprint for a
# live key only silences the alarm for something still exploitable.

# 517627e — a throwaway P-256 key written into the DeviceCheck verify tests so
# pyjwt had something to sign with. Removed in 922805f, which generates the key
# at test time instead and records that no real .p8 ever entered the repo
# (checked by key body and by filename). It signs nothing of ours.
517627ef9152c4d52c6c03a95889f44aeebada2a:backend/tests/test_production.py:private-key:423

# f686498 — two deliberately malformed key shapes (a flattened PEM and a bare
# base64 body) that devicecheck.py must name. Both bodies are the same 48
# characters: the fixed PKCS#8 prefix every P-256 key starts with, cut off
# before the private scalar. There is no key material in either.
f686498bc7dc1c62b95eb2f7c9a7fb61f005ad73:backend/tests/test_production.py:private-key:497
